<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Pōtatau Te Wherowhero</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. <a href="/years/1860">1860</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Pōtatau I</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Pōtatau Te Wherowhero was a leading chief of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/waikato">Waikato</a> tribes and a noted warrior of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/musket">Musket</a> Wars. In 1858 he was crowned at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ngaruawahia">Ngāruawāhia</a> as the first Māori King, chosen by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maori-king-movement">Kīngitanga</a> movement to unite tribes and protect Māori land against sales to settlers. He died in 1860 and was succeeded by his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tawhiao">Tāwhiao</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/potatau-te-wherowhero.jpg" alt="Pōtatau Te Wherowhero" width="320" height="400" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: George French, 1822-1886 Angas (Copyist); J W fl 1847 Giles (as the lithographer); Joseph Jenner, 1816?-1854 Merrett (as the artist) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:P%C5%8Dtatau_Te_Wherowhero_by_George_French_Angas.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Te Wherowhero, as he was first known, was the eldest son of Te Rau-angaanga of the chiefly line of Ngāti Mahuta, who had become the principal war chief of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/waikato">Waikato</a> tribes near the end of the 18th century, and of Te Parengaope of Ngāti Koura. Through them he traced descent from the captains of both the Tainui and Te Arawa canoes. He was taught traditional lore by his father and at the Waikato school of knowledge at Whatawhata, and lived at Kaitotehe pā on the Waikato River.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In the Musket Wars he led 3,000 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/waikato">Waikato</a> and Ngāti Maniapoto warriors against Ngāti Toa at Kāwhia, after which Te Rauparaha's people left for northern Taranaki. Early in 1822 the Waikato were heavily defeated at Motunui, and that year Te Wherowhero commanded the unsuccessful defence of Matakitaki against Ngāpuhi under Hongi Hika, who were armed with muskets. In 1831 he led a war party that killed many hundreds of Ngātiawa, whose lands he later claimed by conquest, and by 1836 he had made peace with the Taranaki tribes.</p><p>He refused to sign the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-waitangi">Treaty of Waitangi</a> when Captain William Symonds brought it to Manukau in March 1840, yet he stayed friendly with the colonial government, above all with Governor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/george-grey">George Grey</a>. In 1846 he protested to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/queen-victoria">Queen Victoria</a> against an edict treating land not occupied or cultivated by Māori as Crown property, and in April 1849 he agreed to provide <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/auckland">Auckland</a> with military protection. From the early 1850s, as settlers encroached on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/waikato">Waikato</a> land, he grew less friendly towards the Pākehā.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>The King Movement sought above all to halt the sale of Māori land. Mātene Te Whiwhi travelled the country looking for a chief of high standing willing to be king; Iwikau Te Heuheu Tūkino III of Ngāti Tūwharetoa proposed Te Wherowhero, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wiremu-tamihana">Wiremu Tāmihana</a> of Ngāti Hauā supported the choice. Elderly and at first reluctant, he agreed at Rangiriri in April 1857 and took the name Pōtatau when he was crowned.</p><p>Pōtatau wished to keep working with the British government, but many of his followers took a more independent position, and the two sides drew apart until war broke out five years later. He died at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ngaruawahia">Ngāruawāhia</a> on 25 June 1860 and was buried on Mount Taupiri. Although he reigned only briefly, he is credited with setting precedents for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maori-king-movement">Kīngitanga</a> that survive today.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Pōtatau Te Wherowhero” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Pōtatau Te Wherowhero</b>, in his acceptance speech, stressed the spirit of unity symbolised by the kingship and called on his people to &quot;hold fast to love, to the law, and to faith in God.&quot;</blockquote><a href="/movement/maori-king-movement">Māori King movement · Aims</a></li><li><blockquote>After long debate, Waikato tribes and their allies chose the Waikato chief <b>Pōtatau Te Wherowhero</b>, crowned at Ngāruawāhia in 1858, with Wiremu Tāmihana as the leading organiser.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingitanga">Kingitanga</a></li><li><blockquote>By 1839 the number of signatories had grown to 52, including the Waikato chief <b>Te Wherowhero</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/united-tribes-of-new-zealand">United Tribes of New Zealand</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Waikato District, in what is now <a href="/countries/new-zealand">New Zealand</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ngaruawahia">Ngāruawāhia</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/new-zealand">New Zealand</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=-37.63,175.21&amp;year=1850" aria-label="Waikato District on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="8926 3517 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Pōtatau Te Wherowhero"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M9376 3787h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M9376 3787h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=-37.63,175.21&amp;year=1850">What was here in 1850? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
